{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1553813-v20-discipline-homebrew","id":1553813,"title":"V20 discipline homebrew","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-09-14T04:08:55-07:00","id":1553813,"title":"V20 discipline homebrew","content":"Has anyone tried to homebrew the discipline rating into discipline rolls? One thing I really liked about requiem (and I think v5 does something similar) . To me I like the affect of increasing capability with all levels of a discipline as the vampire gets better with it instead of being stuck on mortal skills. Don't think we can just add the dice as it might skew things, or just reduce difficulty by discipline rating as higher levels auto get this bonus (though you could argue high level powers are just strong). <br /> Maybe reduce the difficulty of a discipline power equal to discipline rating minus level of power the vampire is trying (reflects a master of a discipline having an easier time with lower level powers).<br /> <br /> Has anyone played around with this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1440-lordofash","name":"LordOfAsh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1440_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-14T13:30:07-07:00","id":1553816,"title":"","content":"I devised some advanced Obfuscate and Presence powers that would reduce the difficulty of the rolls of the basic powers. This is important because statistically characters with maxed out attributes and skills would roll few successes on average, making even the lore about the powers weird, as they're much less effective than one would expect. Reducing the difficulty at higher levels means justifying mechanically elder vampires pulling off the basic powers more impressively and consistently.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-14T20:34:47-07:00","id":1553828,"title":"","content":"I do like Requiem's system, but the key difference between those two takes is that using Attribute + Ability lets you adjust how good a character is with a power separately from how many powers they have access to. That way you can make characters that are really good at the powers they got without the need of giving them more powers, or make characters that have a plethora of options while keeping their dice pools at a manageable size.<br /> <br /> This kind of fine-tuning is overall lost in CoD, including Requiem. It's the trade-off for the benefits the system is built around. I don't think either take is inherently better, but I do think it works better in CoD because of the many other differences in the system and how it interacts with the whole of it.<br /> <br /> But if you want to do it, You just have to exchange either Attribute or Ability for Discipline level. A roll of Attribute + Discipline is more intuitive, but requires two high-cost traits and plays a lot on the idea of personal power as the source of the effects. Ability + Discipline is kinda weird, but lowers the cost for good dice pools and reinforce the idea that effective application derives from knowing what you're doing and how things work.<br /> <br /> Since you're avoiding increasing actual odds beyond what the game already does, that'll keep things mostly in place. Changing effects so they scale with Discipline level is possible, but will take some work. And if you find out you actually <b>want</b> to increase odds, then I think adding dice or reducing difficulty are actually viable approaches. Adding dice is probably the simpler one, then the roll for most Disciplines become Attribute + Ability + Discipline and the odds of success increase a little. It also gives an advantage for the user over anyone resisting, which isn't necessarily a bad thing. Reducing Difficulty is trickier overall.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}